% D-multitarget-roadmap.weft — The consolidation roadmap that must be
% completed before kcc grows a second code-generation target.
%
% The Phase 0--9 roadmap (Appendix, C-roadmap.weft) carries kcc from a
% bootable skeleton to a self-hosting, single-target production compiler.
% Its final open item (#10) is "a second target (x86-64 / ELF)."  This
% appendix is the roadmap for everything that should be finished *first*,
% organized around a single principle so the order is not arbitrary.

\chapter{Roadmap: The Road to a Second Target}
\label{ch:multitarget-roadmap}

% Status glyphs.  Defined with \providecommand so this appendix renders
% whether or not the Phase~9 roadmap (which defines the same names) has
% already been read in the same document.
\providecommand{\stDone}{\textcolor{green!55!black}{\checkmark\,\textsc{done}}}
\providecommand{\stPart}{\textcolor{orange!90!black}{$\odot$\,\textsc{partial}}}
\providecommand{\stOpen}{\textcolor{black!45}{$\circ$\,\textsc{open}}}
\providecommand{\meterA}{$\bullet\bullet\bullet$}
\providecommand{\meterB}{$\bullet\bullet\circ$}
\providecommand{\meterC}{$\bullet\circ\circ$}

\kcc{} today is a correct, self-hosting C17 compiler for one target
(ARM64 / Mach-O) that compiles and runs real software---Lua~5.4.7's
entire official suite, zlib~1.3.1 end to end, cJSON byte-identical to
\texttt{clang}.  The natural next ambition is a \emph{second} target.
This appendix argues that a second target should not be started yet, and
lays out precisely what to finish first.

% ================================================================
\section{The Guiding Principle}
\label{sec:mt-principle}

The retargeting playbook (Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}) establishes that
the IR is the \emph{neutral seam}: roughly seventy percent of the
compiler---the preprocessor, parser, semantic analysis, IR construction,
the optimizer, and the IR verifier---is target-independent and is reused
verbatim by every backend.  Only the last thirty percent (instruction
selection, register banks, ABI, object emission) is target-specific.

That split dictates the order of work by one rule:

\begin{center}
\fcolorbox{black!55}{black!4}{%
\parbox{0.9\linewidth}{\centering\smallskip
\textbf{Finish the shared seventy percent before you split.}\\[3pt]
Every defect in the target-neutral core that survives into a two-target
world costs \emph{twice}: it must be re-diagnosed through the ambiguity
``is this the shared frontend or the new backend?'', and every fix must
be re-validated against two code generators and two test baselines.  A
feature added to the IR optimizer \emph{before} the split is written once
and inherited free by the second target; the same feature added
\emph{after} must be proven correct on both.\smallskip}}
\end{center}

\noindent There is a second, quieter reason.  The ARM64 backend is not
just \emph{a} target---once a second target exists it becomes the
\emph{oracle}: the differential reference (\texttt{clang}-validated,
byte-identical on real code) against which the new backend's output is
checked.  An oracle must be complete and trustworthy before it is relied
upon.  So the pre-split work is of exactly two kinds: \textbf{(A)}
complete and prove the shared core, and \textbf{(B)} polish ARM64 into a
dependable oracle.

% ================================================================
\section{Milestones at a Glance}
\label{sec:mt-glance}

Six milestones (M1--M6) precede the second target (M7).  M1--M4 are
target-neutral (kind~A); M5 is ARM64 oracle-hardening (kind~B); M6 is the
structural work that \emph{enables} the split.  The table is the scan; the
sections are the detail.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@@{}clccl@@{}}
\hline
\textbf{\#} & \textbf{Milestone} & \textbf{Kind} & \textbf{Effort} & \textbf{Status}\\
\hline
M1 & Finish the C17 frontend (designators, volatile bit-fields, real \texttt{float}) & neutral & \meterB & \stDone\\
M2 & Harden the IR verifier                    & neutral & \meterB & \stDone\\
M3 & Systematic conformance battery            & neutral & \meterA & \stDone\\
M4 & Broaden the IR optimizer (strength reduction + copy propagation) & neutral & \meterA & \stDone\\
M5 & Write-protect read-only data (stray writes fault, not corrupt) & oracle & \meterB & \stPart\\
M6 & Carve the target interface (route ARM64 through it) & seam & \meterA & \stOpen\\
\hline
M7 & Second target (x86-64 / ELF)              & backend & \meterA & \stOpen\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent\emph{Effort:}\quad \meterA{}\,high \quad \meterB{}\,medium
\quad \meterC{}\,low.\quad M1--M5 may proceed in parallel; M6 must come
last of the six, because it is validated by re-running the now-complete
M1--M5 suites through the refactored ARM64 path with byte-identical
output.

% ================================================================
\section{M1 --- Finish the C17 Frontend}
\label{sec:mt-m1}

\textbf{Goal.}  Close the remaining language gaps so the shared frontend
is genuinely conforming before it feeds two backends.  Each of these is
IR-\emph{construction} work---it lives above the seam and is reused by
every target.

\textbf{Work items.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nested designated initializers} (\stdref{6.7.9}):
      \texttt{.a.b[2] = v} and brace-elision across aggregate levels.
      Single-level \texttt{.f=}/\texttt{[i]=} is already done; the
      recursion over sub-objects is the gap.
\item \textbf{Volatile bit-fields} \emph{(landed)}: the synthesised
      read--modify--write of a \texttt{volatile}-declared bit-field now stamps
      the volatile bit on its container load and store
      (\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_bf\_container\_type} feeding the type-taking
      \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_emit\_load\_t}/\texttt{\_store\_t}), so CSE and DCE
      treat each access as a mandatory side effect---parity with a volatile
      scalar member.
\item \textbf{A real single-precision \texttt{float} model} \emph{(landed)}.
      Values still live as \texttt{double} in the SIMD registers---a uniform
      backend model---but a binary operation whose \emph{result} type is
      \texttt{float} now appends an \texttt{IR\_FPROUND}, rounding that
      sub-expression to single precision as it is produced.  This gives the
      \texttt{FLT\_EVAL\_METHOD}~$0$ semantics Apple ARM64 and \texttt{clang}
      use---\texttt{a/b*c} matches \texttt{clang} to the last bit---while a
      comparison (\texttt{int} result) and a mixed \texttt{float}/%
      \texttt{double} expression (full precision) are correctly left alone.
      Settling this \emph{now} avoids re-baselining two backends later.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{27b-sema-declarations} and
\texttt{32c-ir-aggregates} (designators); \texttt{32b-ir-expressions}
and \texttt{37c-codegen-dispatch} (volatile bit-fields; the value model
is neutral, the final load/store width is where ARM64 realizes it);
\texttt{28-conversions} and the IR float opcodes (\texttt{float} model).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The differential corpus (\texttt{make difftest})
gains cases for each feature and stays byte-identical to \texttt{clang};
self-host stays byte-identical; no existing suite regresses.

\textbf{Status.}  In progress.  Nested designator chains
(\texttt{.p.a}, \texttt{.arr[1]}, \texttt{[0].x}) and brace elision
(\texttt{int m[2][2] = \{1,2,3,4\}}) are \emph{landed}, at both block and
file scope, through a single recursive initializer
(\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_fill\_list}) shared by the local and global paths and
validated element-for-element against \texttt{clang}.  Volatile-declared bit-fields now carry the volatile bit on their
container access, at parity with volatile scalar members.  Single-precision
\texttt{float} arithmetic now rounds each \texttt{float} sub-expression once
(\texttt{FLT\_EVAL\_METHOD}~$0$), bit-exact with \texttt{clang}.  With this,
\textbf{M1 is complete}; the residual (propagating \texttt{volatile} from a
qualified \emph{object} through member access) is a general member-access
matter, not specific to this milestone.

% ================================================================
\section{M2 --- Harden the IR Verifier}
\label{sec:mt-m2}

\textbf{Goal.}  Make the IR verifier (Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-validation})
strong enough to reject malformed IR \emph{before} it reaches any
backend.  With one code generator, a backend crash and an IR defect look
alike; with two, a verifier that catches the defect at the seam is what
keeps a frontend bug from masquerading as a backend bug.

\textbf{Work items} \emph{(landed)}.  The verifier
(\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_validate}, \S\ref{ch:ir-validation}) now walks every
function, block, and instruction and enforces three invariants whose
violation is undefined back-end behaviour: valid opcodes (an
uninitialised or corrupted \texttt{IR\_OP}---the shape of the
stale-\texttt{NOP} bug---is rejected), terminated blocks (a non-empty
block that would let code generation fall through is rejected), and finite
instruction lists (a cyclic \texttt{IR\_NEXT} is reported, not spun on).
It is wired into the driver as a mandatory pass between the optimizer and
the back end, so it also catches an optimization pass that corrupts the
IR.  Dataflow invariants that need dominance (strict def-before-use) or
that \kcc's pre-register-allocation IR does not maintain (single static
assignment) are deliberately left to a later dataflow-aware pass, to avoid
false rejections.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{33-ir-validation} (real verifier replacing
the stub); \texttt{42-driver} (mandatory call before code generation).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The verifier accepts all IR the current suites
produce---every codegen, difftest, self-host, interop, e2e case, plus all
of Lua and zlib, compiles with no false rejection---and a controlled fault
(tightening the opcode ceiling) confirms it does fire and abort the
compile with a diagnostic naming the function.

\textbf{Status.}  Done.

% ================================================================
\section{M3 --- Systematic Conformance Battery}
\label{sec:mt-m3}

\textbf{Goal.}  Prove the shared frontend and IR are correct on the
\emph{uncommon} cases that no hand-picked real program happened to
exercise---\emph{while there is still only one target}, so a failure
localizes unambiguously to the core.

\textbf{Work items.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{(done)} Run the \texttt{c-testsuite} curated corpus (220
      single-file programs, each with an expected exit code and stdout)
      through \kcc{} as an external oracle, and record a baseline: at the
      start \textbf{181/220} passed (24 compile gaps, 15 miscompiles); after
      the fixes below, \textbf{184/220}, and every bug the corpus surfaced
      that lies in the shared front end or IR was fixed directly.
\item \emph{(future)} A \texttt{csmith} random-differential harness
      (compile with \kcc{} and \texttt{clang}, compare runtime output,
      shrink divergences) is the natural next escalation; it needs the
      external generator installed and is left as a follow-on.
\end{itemize}
Per project policy (\S no-fuzz-harness) this uses \emph{external}
generators as oracles and fixes each surfaced bug directly; it does not add
a bespoke in-tree fuzzing suite.  Each fix instead gains a targeted
differential regression test, and the existing suites stay green.

\textbf{Bugs the corpus surfaced and fixed} (all in the target-neutral
core, so both targets inherit the fix): \textbf{(1)} reaching the closing
brace of \texttt{main} now returns 0 (\stdref{5.1.2.2.3}) instead of a
garbage register; \textbf{(2)} a file-scope floating variable initialised
from an integer constant (\texttt{double d = 100;}) now converts to the
floating representation instead of storing the integer bits; \textbf{(3)}
reading a \texttt{float}/\texttt{double} \emph{global} or struct member now
emits a floating load carrying the real type, not an integer load;
\textbf{(4)} a multi-declarator external declaration (\texttt{int a, b;})
now registers \emph{every} declarator, not just the first.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{21-parser} (multi-declarator tail),
\texttt{32-ir-builder} (main-returns-0, global scalar init conversion),
\texttt{32b-ir-expressions} (floating global/member load type),
\texttt{49-differential-testing} (seven new regression cases).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  A recorded c-testsuite baseline, every
core-attributable divergence fixed with a regression test, and the full
kcc suite plus Lua and zlib still green.  Remaining corpus failures are
tracked feature gaps (variadic \texttt{char} promotion, some predefined
macros, a few control-flow corners), not silent miscompiles.

\textbf{Status.}  Done (c-testsuite corpus); \texttt{csmith} follow-on
deferred.

% ================================================================
\section{M4 --- Broaden the IR Optimizer}
\label{sec:mt-m4}

\textbf{Goal.}  Raise generated-code quality with passes that are
\emph{entirely} IR-to-IR, so the second target inherits every one of
them at no additional cost.  This is the milestone where the ``write
once, reused free'' economics of the guiding principle pay off most.

\textbf{Work items.}  On top of the present constant folding, CSE, and DCE,
the fixed-point pipeline gains two new IR-to-IR passes.

\emph{(landed)} \textbf{Strength reduction and algebraic simplification}
(\texttt{\_kcc\_opt\_strength}, \S\ref{sec:opt-strength}): multiply, unsigned
divide, and unsigned remainder by a power of two become a shift or a mask
(\texttt{x*2\textsuperscript{k}}$\to$\texttt{x<<k},
\texttt{x/\textsubscript{u}2\textsuperscript{k}}$\to$\texttt{x>>\textsubscript{u}k},
\texttt{x\%\textsubscript{u}2\textsuperscript{k}}$\to$\texttt{x\&(2\textsuperscript{k}-1)}),
and \texttt{x*0}/\texttt{x\&0} fold to zero; signed \texttt{SDIV}/\texttt{SMOD}
and every floating operation are deliberately left alone.

\emph{(landed)} \textbf{Copy and constant propagation}
(\texttt{\_kcc\_opt\_copyprop}, \S\ref{ch:cse}): because \kcc{} keeps locals
in memory, ``copies'' live as store/load pairs, so the pass does
\emph{store-to-load forwarding}---a load that reads exactly what a preceding
store wrote takes the stored value (or constant) directly, and DCE removes
the dead load.  It is airtight by three conditions rather than by alias or
dominance analysis: the store and load sit in one straight-line block with no
store, call, or \texttt{va\_start} between them (memory is provably
unchanged); both are the natural 8-byte access (no truncation or re-extension
can perturb the value); and substitution is intra-block (the map resets at
each block boundary).  Constants stored then loaded are forwarded by the same
mechanism, and constant \emph{folding} already propagates within a block
through its value map, so global constant propagation is substantially
covered.  \emph{(future)} Sparse-conditional constant propagation across the
CFG and small-function inlining remain; the inliner (IR cloning, vreg
remapping, param/argument binding, callee-alloca hoisting) is the largest
dataflow addition and is deferred.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{35-peephole} (\texttt{Opt: strength reduction}),
\texttt{36a-cse} (\texttt{\_kcc\_opt\_copyprop}), \texttt{34-ir-passes}
(pipeline), \texttt{assembly.weft} (routing).

\textbf{Acceptance.}  Behaviour preserved on the whole deterministic
corpus---codegen 222, difftest 208 (runtime-equal to \texttt{clang},
including negative signed operands and a store-then-alias-store-then-load
that must \emph{not} forward), self-host 222, and zlib (example self-test and
a gzip round-trip) all green.  Metric: a benchmark of nine multiplies, three
divides, and four remainders by powers of two compiles to \textbf{zero}
\texttt{mul} and \texttt{udiv} machine instructions.  (The Lua suite, an
additional oracle, is presently blocked by an environment-dependent flake
unrelated to these passes---the historical commit that first passed it
fails identically today---so the deterministic suites and zlib stand in.)

\textbf{Status.}  \textbf{Done.}  Both IR-to-IR passes have landed---strength
reduction and copy \& constant propagation---and the acceptance metric is met:
the benchmark of multiplies, unsigned divides, and unsigned remainders by
powers of two compiles to \emph{zero} \texttt{mul} and \texttt{udiv} machine
instructions (each becomes a shift or a mask), with the deterministic corpus,
self-host, and zlib all green and the Lua suite (its layout flake now
non-reproducing) again passing as an extra oracle.  Sparse-conditional constant
propagation across the CFG and function inlining are tracked as \emph{future
escalations beyond this milestone}---valuable, but not open parts of M4's
definition of done, exactly as the \texttt{csmith} harness is a deferred
follow-on to M3 rather than a gap in it.

% ================================================================
\section{M5 --- Write-Protect Read-Only Data (Make Stray Writes Fault)}
\label{sec:mt-m5}

\textbf{Kind.}  This is the roadmap's first \emph{kind-B} milestone: it does
not extend the shared core but polishes ARM64 into a trustworthy oracle, by
converting a whole class of \emph{silent} miscompile into an immediate,
deterministic fault.  An oracle that can corrupt data without complaint is not
yet dependable enough to validate a second backend against.

\textbf{The defect.}  \kcc's object emitter places \emph{all} program data into
a single writable \texttt{\_\_common} section in \texttt{\_\_DATA}.  A build of
Lua makes this concrete: the \texttt{static const} lookup tables of
\texttt{luaL\_checkoption} (\texttt{opts[]}/\texttt{optsnum[]}), ordinary
mutable globals, and zero-initialised BSS all share one writable segment
(\texttt{vmaddr 0x100134000}, one \texttt{\_\_common} block), with no
\texttt{\_\_DATA\_CONST}/\texttt{\_\_const} for read-only statics and no
distinct \texttt{\_\_bss}.  Nothing that should be read-only is write-protected.
This is what turns every codegen \emph{stray write}---the recurring bug family
of an undersized \texttt{alloca} (Bug~38), a mis-coloured spill slot (Bug~32),
or an 8-byte load/store through a 4-byte slot (Bug~45)---from a hard fault into
a \emph{silent} corruption: the wild store lands on a neighbouring object and is
discovered only much later, as scrambled data, at a site arbitrarily far from
the cause.

\textbf{The exemplar (Lua \texttt{all.lua:109}).}  Under \texttt{\_port=true},
\texttt{collectgarbage("count")} intermittently returns \texttt{boolean false}
instead of a number, and the entire \texttt{collectgarbage} option dispatch is
scrambled (\texttt{"collect"}$\to$\texttt{nil}, \texttt{"count"}$\to$%
\texttt{false}, \texttt{"isrunning"}$\to$a number) while every unrelated
operation---\texttt{math.floor}, \texttt{string.format},
\texttt{("count"):len()}---stays correct.  Disassembling the two functions
proves the dispatch \emph{code} is right: it calls \texttt{luaL\_checkoption},
indexes \texttt{optsnum[idx]} with an \texttt{ldrsw}, and switches on the result
with a correct if-else chain.  So the fault is not logic; it is a foreign write
onto the \texttt{opts[]}/\texttt{optsnum[]} tables the path reads.  And it is
\emph{layout-sensitive}: \kcc's build is deterministic, yet whether the stray
write collides with those tables depends on the final binary layout, so shifting
any single translation unit---or merely changing the linker---moves the
collision and flips the bug on or off.  The commit that historically first
passed the whole Lua suite reproduces the failure in some environments and
passes it in others (under both \kcc's own linker and \texttt{clang}'s ld
today); the current build passes.  A heisenbug of this shape resists the
ordinary tools: any injected \texttt{printf} or assert perturbs the layout and
masks it, and it cannot be reproduced on demand.  \emph{Write protection is what
makes it catchable}---with the tables in a read-only page, the offending store
faults at its own instruction and \texttt{lldb} names the culprit function on
the spot, instead of the investigator chasing distant scrambled data.

\textbf{Mechanism (what reconnaissance established).}  The fix is larger than a
section retag, because of two facts about \kcc's back end.  First, \kcc{} emits a
single \texttt{MH\_OBJECT} and \emph{delegates final linking to the system
\texttt{ld}}; the read-only page protection of \texttt{\_\_DATA\_CONST} (the
\texttt{SG\_READ\_ONLY} segment flag) is therefore \texttt{ld}'s job, not
\kcc's.  \kcc{} only has to \emph{name} the section (\texttt{\_\_TEXT,\_\_const}
or \texttt{\_\_DATA\_CONST,\_\_const}) that \texttt{ld} already places in a
read-only segment---there is no segment-protection code to write.  Second, and
harder: \kcc{} stores \emph{no} initialiser bytes in the object.  The
\texttt{\_\_common} section is \texttt{S\_ZEROFILL}, and \emph{every} global
initialiser---scalars and aggregates alike---is executed at load time as runtime
stores from a synthesised \texttt{kcc.module\_init} constructor.  A store into a
read-only page faults, so a \texttt{const} object cannot be both runtime-stored
and write-protected.  Making it read-only therefore requires \emph{also}
laying its initialiser bytes into a real (\texttt{S\_REGULAR}) file-backed
section at compile time and suppressing its \texttt{module\_init} stores.  A
third fact eases one worry: \texttt{const} is currently dropped in semantics
(\S\ref{ch:ir-validation} neighbours), but it can be read straight from the
declaration AST at global pre-registration, so the shared type descriptors---and
the identity comparisons that depend on them---stay untouched.

\textbf{Work items (staged).}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Stage~1.}  A \texttt{const}-qualified static whose initialiser is a
      non-relocatable compile-time constant (integer/float scalars and
      aggregates of them---the \texttt{optsnum[]} victim class) is emitted into
      read-only \texttt{\_\_TEXT} \emph{(landed)}.  Rather than mint a new
      \texttt{section\_64}, it reuses the existing read-only tail that already
      carries string literals (the growable \texttt{\_kcc\_cstr\_buf} appended to
      \texttt{\_\_text}): its bytes are evaluated at compile time from the
      literal initialiser, a section-1 symbol is minted at that offset with a
      fixup (value patched to $\mathit{code\_size}+\mathit{offset}$) and an
      extern-table entry so every \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} dedups to it, and its
      \texttt{module\_init} stores are suppressed.  A stray write into such a
      table now faults (\texttt{SIGBUS}) at the store instead of silently
      corrupting a neighbour.  Structs, floats, and non-literal constant
      initialisers still take the writable path and are a later slice.
\item \textbf{Stage~2.}  A \texttt{const} static whose initialiser contains
      address relocations (e.g.\ \texttt{static const char *const opts[]}) goes
      to \texttt{\_\_DATA\_CONST,\_\_const}, where \texttt{ld}/dyld apply the
      pointer fix-ups and \emph{then} make the page read-only.
\item \textbf{Stage~3 (optional).}  Split zero-initialised BSS from initialised
      data so a BSS overrun cannot walk into a live object, and add a debug
      canary / guard-page mode around static tables.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{37-codegen} (\texttt{const} detection at
\texttt{\_kcc\_preregister\_globals} + a new compile-time initialiser-to-bytes
data buffer), \texttt{40-linker-info} (per-symbol section decision in
\texttt{\_kcc\_linker\_global\_sym}, a new \texttt{\_kcc\_rodata\_size} beside
\texttt{\_kcc\_globals\_size}), \texttt{39-emit-macho} (the new
\texttt{section\_64} header, its real file bytes, and the load-command/file-offset
arithmetic), \texttt{32-ir-builder} (suppress the \texttt{module\_init}/%
\texttt{main} init stores for a const object).  Note: \emph{not} segment
protection---the system \texttt{ld} owns that.

\textbf{Acceptance.}  A \texttt{static const} object lives in a read-only page
(a deliberate write to one faults); the whole deterministic corpus---codegen,
difftest, self-host---plus Lua and zlib stay green (re-baselined, since moving
data to a new section legitimately changes the emitted bytes); and a controlled
stray write into a \texttt{const} table now aborts at the store, with
\texttt{lldb} reporting the writing instruction, instead of surfacing as
distant corruption.

\textbf{Status.}  Partial---Stage~1 landed.  A \texttt{const} integer scalar or
array with a literal initialiser is now write-protected: its bytes live in
read-only \texttt{\_\_TEXT}, a laundered write to one faults with \texttt{SIGBUS}
at the store, and the whole corpus stays green (codegen~222, difftest~213 with
five new \texttt{rodata-*} cases, self-host~222, Lua \texttt{final OK} with
\texttt{optsnum[]} now in read-only memory, zlib self-test).  Multi-TU
\texttt{extern const} references and the \texttt{module\_init} path are verified.
Remaining: Stage~1's struct/float/non-literal cases, then Stage~2
(pointer-const $\to$ \texttt{\_\_DATA\_CONST}) and the optional Stage~3.  Being
kind~B, this milestone may proceed in parallel with M1--M4 and need not precede
the seam refactor---but doing it \emph{early} would have turned three
past heisenbugs (Bugs~32, 38, 45) into one-line faults at the store site, which
is the standing argument for scheduling oracle-hardening sooner rather than
later.

% ================================================================
\section{M6 --- Carve the Target Interface}
\label{sec:mt-m6}

\textbf{Goal.}  Perform the structural refactor that turns ``the ARM64
backend'' into ``the first instance of a target interface,'' so a second
target slots in beside it rather than forking the backend.  This is the
step that literally \emph{enables} M7.

\textbf{Work items.}  Following the retargeting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}): express register banks as
\emph{descriptors} (so the linear-scan allocator becomes
bank-parameterized rather than ARM64-wired), lift the ABI into a
\emph{table} (argument/return classification, callee-saved sets,
alignment), and route instruction selection, prologue/epilogue, and
object emission through a \emph{target vtable}.  Add the
\texttt{-{}-target} selection plumbing to the driver.  Throughout, ARM64
remains the sole concrete target and must keep passing through the new
indirection.

\textbf{weft files.}  \texttt{37/37a/37b/37c-codegen} (behind the
vtable), \texttt{38/39-emit} and \texttt{40-linker} (object-format
parameterization), \texttt{42-driver} (\texttt{-{}-target}); the
interface itself is a new backend-infrastructure fragment.

\textbf{Acceptance.}  The \emph{whole} suite---most importantly self-host
and \texttt{difftest}---stays \textbf{byte-identical} after the refactor:
a pure structural change to ARM64's path must produce the exact same
bytes it did before.  This is why M6 comes last of the six: it is
validated by re-running the by-now-complete M1--M5 suites through the
refactored path.

\textbf{Status.}  Open.  The playbook (Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting})
already specifies the interface shape; this milestone is its
implementation for ARM64.

% ================================================================
\section{M7 --- The Second Target (Hand-off)}
\label{sec:mt-m7}

With M1--M6 complete, adding x86-64 / Mach-O (then ELF / Linux) is the
thirty-percent problem the playbook describes: an instruction-selection
table, a register-bank descriptor, an ABI table, and an object emitter,
validated differentially against the ARM64 oracle and run under
Rosetta~/ qemu.  It is deliberately \emph{out of scope} for this
appendix, whose entire purpose is to guarantee that when M7 begins, every
failure it uncovers belongs to the new backend and nowhere else.  The
mechanics of M7 live in the retargeting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}).

% ================================================================
\section{Summary}
\label{sec:mt-summary}

The order is not a preference; it falls out of the seam.  M1--M4 finish
and prove the target-neutral seventy percent while a single target still
makes every failure unambiguous.  M5 write-protects read-only data so the
ARM64 oracle turns stray writes into hard faults at the store instead of
silent, layout-sensitive corruption---the oracle must not lie before it is
trusted to judge a second backend.  M6 carves the interface with ARM64 as
the only occupant, proven by byte-identical output.  Only then does M7
introduce a second occupant---by which point the shared core is complete,
the optimizer is inherited free, the verifier guards the seam, and ARM64
is a trustworthy oracle.  \emph{Finish the shared part before you split};
the rest is instruction tables.
